## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## review_pr — "Revi", a read-only cross-family code reviewer.
##
## Revi reviews the changes on the current branch and PUBLISHES the
## findings — it never edits, fixes, or commits code. That mutation
## job belongs to the improve-loops (Billy = branch_improve_loop,
## Willy = whole_improve_loop). Revi is the PR reviewer: it reads the
## diff, reasons about it with one reviewer (mono, the default) or two
## independent reviewers from different model families (dual — opt-in via
## `--var review_mode=dual`), then a single emit step
## merges + de-duplicates their findings and writes them to the
## iterion native kanban board (one issue per finding) plus a markdown
## report.
##
## Shape (single pass — no loop, no convergence, nothing to converge
## ON since Revi changes nothing between iterations):
##
##   diff_precheck -> tier_expand (deterministic: resolve review_tier
##                     into severity_threshold / max_findings /
##                     post_to_board / effective_review_mode — #685)
##   topology (condition — ADR-052 + review tiers)
##     ├─ mono, guard tier (DEFAULT) -> reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt
##     ├─ mono, glance tier          -> reviewer_claude_glance /
##                                      reviewer_gpt_glance (cheaper
##                                      same-family model)
##     └─ dual (review_mode=dual, or audit tier which forces it)
##                       -> fan (fan_out_all)
##                            ├─ reviewer_claude  (claude_code, read-only)
##                            └─ reviewer_gpt     (claude_code + zai/glm-5.3)
##   reviewer_* -> merge_reviews -> converge   (await: wait_all)
##                 board + markdown report
##   converge -> pr_gate   (deterministic: was a pr_url given?)
##     ├─ no  -> done
##     └─ yes -> publish_review  (DETERMINISTIC forge publish via the
##               iterion server endpoint — no LLM, no workspace token) ->
##               publish_health  (deterministic anti-façade gate) -> done
##
## REVIEW TIERS (#685) — glance / guard / audit — let a repo's criticality
## or budget policy pick a preset for the knobs above in ONE var
## (`review_tier`) instead of five, pinnable per repo through the
## integration's launch_vars (durable across re-provisioning). guard is
## the DEFAULT and is byte-identical to the bot's pre-#685 posture. See
## `tier_expand` and each sentinel-default var's own doc for the full
## contract — the tier is a preset, never a cage: any of the underlying
## vars stays individually overridable via its own --var.
##
## Cross-family value (DUAL only): an issue flagged by BOTH families is
## far more likely real than one flagged by a single reviewer. emit raises
## the confidence of cross-confirmed findings and labels them so the
## operator can triage agreement first. It is worth paying for on a
## high-stakes diff, not on every routine push — hence mono by default
## (and exactly the deliberate spend the "audit" tier opts into).
## In mono every finding is still reported; only the cross-confirmation
## tag is absent.
##
## Scope discipline (matches the loop bots): reviewers diff the
## WORKING TREE against the branch point, so both committed and
## uncommitted branch changes are reviewed. That base is resolved ONCE by
## `diff_precheck` — which refreshes `base_ref` from the remote first, then
## takes the merge-base — and published as `base_sha`; every consumer reads
## that sha instead of recomputing one from a ref name, because a checkout
## reused across runs carries a stale `base_ref` and the merge-base against
## it lands below the branch point. To review ONLY the uncommitted working
## tree, run with `--var base_ref=HEAD` (nothing is fetched, the merge-base
## is HEAD, and the diff is HEAD-vs-working-tree).
##
## Read-only safety: no node mutates source. reviewer_claude is
## `readonly: true`; reviewer_gpt is given only read tools (bash,
## read_file, glob, grep — no write_file/file_edit). The single
## downstream `emit` step writes exactly one file (the report) and
## creates board issues over MCP; it runs after the parallel reviewers
## converge, so the one-mutating-branch workspace rule holds.
##
## TICKET CONFORMANCE (v0.6.0 — opt-in, tracker-agnostic): when
## `tracker_api_base` is set (typically pinned per repo via the
## integration's launch_vars) and a `tracker_token` secret is bound,
## the reviewers ALSO fetch the ticket(s) the PR references (explicit
## `ticket_refs`, or extracted from the PR title/body + branch name)
## and verify the diff answers the demand. Gaps are findings of
## category "requirements" (they gate like any finding); a per-ticket
## verdict (covered / partial / not covered / unverifiable) lands in
## the markdown report AND the PR review summary. Tracker specifics
## (Jira Cloud/DC, GitHub, GitLab, auth recipes) live in
## skills/ticket-context.md — never in this DSL (universal-bots
## doctrine). Fetch failure degrades to an explicit "unverifiable",
## never a silent skip and never a failed review.
##
## v2 opportunity: very large diffs can overflow a reviewer's context.
## The loop bots solve this with a deterministic `plan_chunks` shard
## step; Revi v1 instructs reviewers to triage `git diff --stat` first
## and prioritise the highest-impact hunks. Port plan_chunks here when
## reviewing 1k+ LoC PRs becomes common.
##
## FORGE PR REVIEW (v0.3.0 — deterministic, tokenless-in-workspace):
## When `--var pr_url=<PR url>` is given, Revi ALSO publishes its
## merged findings directly onto that PR as a proper forge review —
## inline comments anchored to file:line, including one-click
## suggestion blocks (when a finding carries a concrete `replacement`)
## — on top of the native kanban board + markdown report it always
## writes. Publishing is a DETERMINISTIC tool node: it POSTs the
## findings to the iterion server's /api/v1/forge/publish-review
## endpoint (authenticated by a per-run token the server injects as
## `forge_publish_url` / `forge_publish_token` launch vars), and the
## SERVER posts the review through the team forge connection's LIVE
## client — a GitHub App connection mints a fresh installation token
## per call, so the ~1h token-freeze failure mode of a workspace-
## mounted forge_token is structurally gone, and no LLM agent ever
## holds a forge credential to post. Forge-agnostic: the endpoint
## dispatches by the connection's provider (GitHub / GitLab /
## Forgejo-Gitea) — no forge names in this DSL (universal-bots
## doctrine). A deterministic `publish_health` gate flags a no-op /
## failed publish (findings existed but 0 comments landed) so a façade
## is never silent. `post_to_board` remains the local sink; both run.
##
## Remaining enhancement (not yet built): auto-resolve base/head from
## the PR URL. Today review the LOCAL checkout (base_ref..HEAD) and
## publish to pr_url — so check out the PR branch and pass its base as
## base_ref. See the `pr_url` var doc below. Manual launch against a PR
## (`--var pr_url=<url>`) works today; a PR-event trigger
## (dispatcher/webhook auto-launch on PR open/update) is the other
## remaining enhancement.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

dsl: 2

import "lib/schemas.bot"
import "lib/prompts.bot"
import "lib/nodes.bot"

secrets:
  ## Forge API token for OTHER forge uses (e.g. cloning a private repo on
  ## a cloud runner). NOT used by the publish path any more: since
  ## v0.3.0 the review is posted by the DETERMINISTIC publish_review
  ## node through the iterion server's forge-publish endpoint, which
  ## uses the team connection's live client — a workspace-mounted token
  ## freezes at launch (a GitHub App installation token lives ~1h) and
  ## must never be the posting credential. Mounted read-only as a FILE
  ## (path/env only, never read into a prompt); `optional: true` so
  ## runs without a binding are unaffected.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

  ## Issue-tracker API credential for TICKET CONFORMANCE (v0.6.0): a
  ## read-only token the reviewers use to fetch the ticket(s) a PR claims
  ## to implement (Jira, GitHub/GitLab issues, ... — tracker knowledge
  ## lives in skills/ticket-context.md, never in this DSL). Mounted as a
  ## FILE: the agent passes the PATH to curl (`$(cat ...)` inside its own
  ## shell), the value never enters a prompt, and the egress guard keeps
  ## it from leaving toward any host but the binding's AllowedHosts (bind
  ## it with AllowedHosts = the tracker instance host). `optional: true`:
  ## teams without a tracker binding are unaffected — the whole feature
  ## stays dormant unless `tracker_api_base` is also set.
  tracker_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Branch comparison base. Reviewers audit
  ## `git diff $(git merge-base base_ref HEAD)` (working tree vs the
  ## merge-base), so committed + uncommitted branch changes are both in
  ## scope. Default "main" covers the common GitHub/GitLab/Forgejo
  ## integration-branch name; pass `--var base_ref=develop` for another
  ## base, or `--var base_ref=HEAD` to review only uncommitted changes.
  base_ref: string = "main"

  ## Free-text steering for the reviewers (e.g. "focus on the new auth
  ## middleware" or "this is a hotfix, prioritise correctness"). Empty
  ## = review the whole diff against the full production-ready grid.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## REVIEW TIER (#685) — a repo's criticality/budget policy, one knob
  ## instead of five. "guard" is the DEFAULT and reproduces the bot's
  ## historical (pre-0.8.0) posture byte-for-byte. "glance" is frugal: a
  ## cheaper same-family reviewer model, a high drafting floor, a small
  ## finding cap, no board writes, and a prompt instruction to skip
  ## exploratory reads beyond the diff — the measured cost floor for a
  ## small PR is what the reviewer ingests before it reads a single diff
  ## line (native issue #685's cost-model comment), so glance's biggest
  ## lever is ingesting LESS, not merely capping the OUTPUT. "audit" is
  ## premium: forces the cross-family dual fan-out regardless of
  ## review_mode, a low drafting floor, and a raised finding cap — a
  ## deliberate spend for a high-stakes diff.
  ##
  ## The tier is a PRESET, not a cage: severity_threshold, max_findings,
  ## post_to_board and review_mode all stay individually overridable via
  ## their own --var — an explicit value on any of them wins over the
  ## tier's default for that one knob (see each var's own doc below and
  ## the deterministic `tier_expand` step that resolves them). Budget
  ## (max_cost_usd/max_duration) is NOT tier-varied: the workflow budget
  ## block is a compile-time DSL literal with its own CLI-flag override
  ## path, disjoint from the vars a tier can reach — re-budget a tier
  ## directly with --max-cost-usd/--max-duration when the default ceiling
  ## (sized off the measured guard-tier median) is not the one you want.
  review_tier: string [enum: "glance", "guard", "audit"] = "guard"

  ## What a fixer already did with an EARLIER review of this same PR, per
  ## finding id: fixed (with the commit), contested (with its argument), or
  ## deferred. Stamped at launch by the engine when such a run exists; empty
  ## otherwise, and empty is the normal case.
  ##
  ## This is what keeps a review↔fix pair converging instead of oscillating: a
  ## finding the fixer argued against comes back only against NEW evidence. It
  ## is NOT an instruction to drop it — a wrong argument must be answered, and
  ## a finding that is still real still counts against the gate.
  prior_pushback: string = ""

  ## TICKET CONFORMANCE (v0.6.0). Base URL of the issue-tracker API the
  ## PR's tickets live on (e.g. "https://jira.example.org" or
  ## "https://myorg.atlassian.net"). Empty (default) = the feature is OFF
  ## and reviews behave exactly as before. When set, the reviewers fetch
  ## the referenced ticket(s) (see skills/ticket-context.md) and verify
  ## the diff actually answers the demand — gaps surface as findings of
  ## category "requirements" and a per-ticket verdict lands in the report
  ## and the PR review summary. Typically pinned per repo via the
  ## integration's launch_vars, not per run.
  tracker_api_base: string = ""

  ## Tracker auth mode hint. Empty = send the token as a Bearer header
  ## (Jira Server/DC personal access tokens, most REST APIs). Non-empty =
  ## HTTP Basic with this value as the username and the token as the
  ## password (Jira Cloud API tokens use the service account's email).
  ## Only meaningful when tracker_api_base is set.
  tracker_user: string = ""

  ## Explicit ticket references to check, space-separated (e.g.
  ## "PROJ-123 PROJ-456"). Empty = the reviewers extract references
  ## themselves from the PR title/body (scope_notes) and the source
  ## branch name, per skills/ticket-context.md.
  ticket_refs: string = ""

  ## The ticket-conformance switch (v0.9.0). "auto" (default) checks the
  ## PR against its ticket(s) whenever they are readable:
  ##   - an EXTERNAL tracker when `tracker_api_base` is set (Jira & co),
  ##   - else the FORGE'S OWN issues, with no configuration at all: the
  ##     API base is derived from `pr_url` and the run already carries a
  ##     forge credential (`forge_token`, bound automatically when the
  ##     repo was provisioned).
  ## Requiring a per-repo var to read an issue that sits on the very
  ## forge the run is already authenticated against was an artificial
  ## limit — a bot that reviews a PR saying "Fixes #123" should read #123.
  ## Set "off" to skip the check entirely (cost, or a repo whose issues
  ## are noise).
  ticket_context: string [enum: "auto", "off"] = "auto"

  ## The PR's head branch name — a GENERIC PR-context var the iterion
  ## webhook lanes inject on every PR launch (empty on a bare CLI run).
  ## Declared here so ticket references embedded in the branch name
  ## (feature/PROJ-123-...) are extractable for ticket conformance.
  source_branch: string = ""

  ## Findings below this severity are not WRITTEN (order: low < medium <
  ## high < critical). Applied at the SOURCE — the reviewers are told not
  ## to draft detail/replacement for anything under the floor (a token
  ## spend the merge step would throw away) — and re-applied post-hoc by
  ## the merge step as a second net.
  ##
  ## SENTINEL "auto" (the default): the `tier_expand` step below resolves
  ## the effective floor from `review_tier` — "high" on glance (today's
  ## "medium"), "low" on audit, "medium" on guard (BYTE-IDENTICAL to the
  ## bot's pre-#685 hardcoded default). Any concrete value here — `--var
  ## severity_threshold=low`, still the everything-mode escape hatch — is
  ## an explicit override and wins over the tier for this one knob.
  ##
  ## This is the floor the MERGE GATE counts above, because nothing under
  ## it reaches the gate — so keep the EFFECTIVE value at or below
  ## `gate_severity` (see there, unaffected by tiers). A deployment that
  ## gates on "low" must lower this one too (or pick a tier that does), or
  ## its required check silently stops seeing what it gates on.
  severity_threshold: string [enum: "auto", "low", "medium", "high", "critical"] = "auto"

  ## Cap the number of board issues / report rows (highest severity
  ## first). A capped run says so prominently in the report so it is
  ## never mistaken for "only N issues exist".
  ##
  ## SENTINEL 0 (the default): `tier_expand` resolves the effective cap
  ## from `review_tier` — 5 on glance, 40 on audit (the pre-0.7.0
  ## "everything" cap), 15 on guard (identical to the bot's pre-#685
  ## hardcoded default). Any positive value here is an explicit override
  ## (a 0 cap is never a real request — it would suppress every finding)
  ## and wins over the tier.
  max_findings: int = 0

  ## When true, emit creates one native-board issue per kept finding
  ## (label source:revi). When false, emit writes the markdown report
  ## only — useful for a read-only PR comment workflow with no board.
  ##
  ## SENTINEL "auto" (the default): `tier_expand` resolves it from
  ## `review_tier` — "false" on glance (a quick signal has no business
  ## filing board issues), "true" on guard/audit (today's default,
  ## unchanged). "true"/"false" here are explicit overrides that win over
  ## the tier — e.g. the webhook lane's own `post_to_board: "false"`
  ## launch var (PR-comment workflows never want board writes, on any
  ## tier) keeps working unchanged.
  post_to_board: string [enum: "auto", "true", "false"] = "auto"

  ## Markdown report destination, repo-root-relative. A neutral
  ## dotfile the operator can gitignore — deliberately NOT under
  ## .iterion/ so Revi never scatters engine state into the target
  ## repo's tree.
  report_path: string = ".review-pr/findings.md"

  ## FORGE PR REVIEW (v0.3.0). When set to a pull-request (merge-request on GitLab) URL,
  ## Revi ALSO publishes its merged findings onto that PR as a real
  ## forge review: one inline comment per finding anchored to file:line,
  ## with a one-click suggestion block when the finding carries a
  ## concrete `replacement`, else a plain inline comment. Empty (default)
  ## = today's board + markdown-report behaviour only.
  ##
  ## Publishing is deterministic + server-mediated: the publish_review
  ## tool node POSTs the findings to the iterion server's
  ## /api/v1/forge/publish-review endpoint, which posts through the
  ## team forge connection's live client (GitHub / GitLab /
  ## Forgejo-Gitea). It needs `forge_publish_url` + `forge_publish_token`
  ## below — injected automatically by the server at launch.
  ##
  ## v1 diff model: review the LOCAL checkout (base_ref..HEAD, as
  ## always) and publish to this PR. So check out the PR's branch
  ## locally and pass its base as `base_ref` (e.g.
  ## `--var base_ref=main --var pr_url=https://github.com/o/r/pull/42`).
  ## Auto-resolving base/head from the PR URL is a future enhancement.
  pr_url: string = ""

  ## Review posting mode when pr_url is set: "inline" (default — one
  ## comment per finding anchored to file:line) or "summary" (a single
  ## rolled-up review body carrying all findings). The endpoint
  ## auto-falls back to a summary-only review when the forge rejects
  ## the inline anchors, so "inline" is safe unattended.
  pr_review_mode: string [enum: "inline", "summary"] = "inline"

  ## REVIEW TOPOLOGY (ADR-052). "mono" runs ONE family reviewer; "dual"
  ## runs both and cross-confirms. Dual costs a full reviewer pass per
  ## family on EVERY review — and with the merge gate wired, every push
  ## re-reviews — so mono is the default and dual is a deliberate opt-in
  ## (`--var review_mode=dual`, or the launch flag). "auto" is resolved at
  ## LAUNCH by pkg/reviewtopology from the host's detected credentials and
  ## also lands on mono; it only picks the family for you.
  ##
  ## Cross-family confirmation is worth paying for on a high-stakes diff,
  ## not on every routine push: a finding either reviewer raises is still
  ## reported, dual only adds the `cross-confirmed by both reviewers` tag.
  ##
  ## `review_tier: "audit"` forces the dual fan-out regardless of what this
  ## resolves to (see `tier_expand`'s `effective_review_mode` below) — dual
  ## IS audit's defining spend. An explicit `--var review_mode=dual` on any
  ## tier also forces it, unchanged from before #685.
  review_mode: string [enum: "auto", "mono", "dual"] = "mono"

  ## Which family runs in mono. Overwritten by the launch-time resolver
  ## when it detects only one usable family (e.g. an Anthropic-forfait
  ## instance with no OpenAI quota resolves to "claude").
  mono_family: string [enum: "claude", "gpt"] = "claude"

  ## MERGE GATE (v0.5.0). When pr_url is set, the deterministic publish
  ## step ALSO posts a commit status named "revi/review" on the PR head
  ## SHA: `success` when 0 findings meet `gate_severity`, else `failure`.
  ## Add that context to the repo's required checks (branch protection /
  ## merge queue) to make Revi's verdict BLOCK the merge; leave it out and
  ## the status is a harmless advisory check. The verdict is DETERMINISTIC
  ## — a count of findings at/above the threshold, computed here, never an
  ## LLM judgment. Non-blocking `questions` never count. A push re-reviews
  ## (opt-in ReviewOnSync webhook) so the status tracks the fixed head.
  ## Set false to skip the status entirely (advisory-only reviews).
  gate_enabled: bool = true

  ## Severity floor that BLOCKS the merge (order: low < medium < high <
  ## critical). Default "high": high + critical findings block; low +
  ## medium advise only. A finding you "would block a PR on" is exactly
  ## the reviewer's own bar (see review_system), so high is the sane floor.
  ##
  ## KEEP IT AT OR ABOVE the EFFECTIVE severity_threshold (see there —
  ## resolved per `review_tier` unless overridden). The gate counts the
  ## findings that SURVIVED the review floor — the reviewers do not draft
  ## below it and converge re-drops anything under it — so a gate_severity
  ## below the effective threshold can never see the findings it claims to
  ## block on, and the check goes green with nothing to say. Lowering this
  ## one means lowering severity_threshold with it (e.g. `--var
  ## gate_severity=low --var severity_threshold=low`).
  ##
  ## NOT tier-varied (deliberately): every tier keeps the same blocking bar
  ## by default — a tier changes what gets DRAFTED and REPORTED, never what
  ## BLOCKS a merge unasked. audit's lower severity_threshold surfaces more
  ## low/medium findings as advisory PR comments without silently making
  ## them merge-blocking; tighten gate_severity yourself when a repo wants
  ## audit to also block on them.
  gate_severity: string [enum: "low", "medium", "high", "critical"] = "high"

  ## The commit-status CONTEXT the gate posts under — the name that appears
  ## in the repo's required checks. A required check applies to EVERY PR, so
  ## a repo where different bots review different PRs (a dependency guard on
  ## the bot's PRs, this reviewer on the humans') must give them ONE shared
  ## context, or whichever bot did not run leaves the check permanently
  ## absent and blocks the PR. Set it per repo via the integration's
  ## launch_vars (durable across re-provisioning), the same value on every
  ## bot that can gate. The default keeps this bot's historical name.
  gate_context: string = "revi/review"

  ## The fixer escalation this repo offers, published with the findings so a
  ## developer reads it where they read them. Empty — the default — omits the
  ## line entirely: a repo with no fixer, or one whose zero-touch lane is
  ## paused, must not be instructed by the gate to invoke one. The reviewer
  ## cannot know which repo it is reviewing, so the repo says.
  ## `{finding}` is replaced by the first finding's id; a hint without it is
  ## published as written. Set it per repo via the integration's launch_vars,
  ## the same place gate_context is set.
  fixer_hint: string = ""

  ## Deterministic forge publish grant — INJECTED BY THE ITERION SERVER
  ## at launch when the run's team has a forge connection covering
  ## pr_url's repo (studio/API launches and inbound webhooks both
  ## inject). forge_publish_url is the server's
  ## /api/v1/forge/publish-review endpoint; forge_publish_token is an
  ## ephemeral per-run token scoped to posting reviews on that one repo
  ## (24h TTL). When left empty (e.g. a bare CLI run with no server),
  ## publish_review skips with an explicit reason and publish_health
  ## banners it — never a silent no-op, and never a fallback to
  ## workspace credentials.
  forge_publish_url: string = ""
  forge_publish_token: string = ""

## ─── Supervisor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

## COST PACER (v0.7.0): a cheap concurrent watchdog on the reviewer
## node(s). Its one job is pace — when the review drags (sprawling
## exploration, re-reads, long silence without finalisation) it posts a
## single "wrap up now" steering message the reviewer picks up at its
## next turn. It never judges code and never adds findings. Cost is
## bounded by design: ≤12 haiku evals on a 6m cooldown (~cents/run) — a
## window that still exists when spend nears the wall: with 4 evals on 5m
## the coordinator refused every wake ("eval budget exhausted") long
## before a $32 trigger could match on a review that runs 40-60 minutes.
## The cost_gt monitor rides the debounced mid-node usage_progress
## samples (plus budget_warning), so it fires WHILE the review runs —
## the cooldown wakes cover pacing between samples.
supervisor pacer:
  watches: [reviewer_claude, reviewer_gpt]
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_SUPERVISOR:-anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5}"
  system: pacer_policy
  cooldown: "6m"
  max_evals: 12
  ## An ABSOLUTE dollar trigger, kept at ~2/3 of the cap (it was 8 of 12):
  ## calibrated lower it fires in the cheap early phase, spends its four
  ## evals telling a review to wrap up before it has reproduced anything,
  ## and is silent by the time spend nears the real wall.
  monitors: ["cost_gt=32"]

prompt pacer_policy:
  You pace a READ-ONLY code reviewer that has a hard cost budget. Its
  review is worthless unless it is DELIVERED — the budget can kill the
  run before the structured output lands.

  Stay SILENT (decision: watch) while the reviewer is making steady,
  scoped progress: reading the diff, checking callers of changed code,
  drafting findings.

  Intervene with ONE short message when you see budget-burning drift:
    - re-reading files it already read, or sweeping the repo far
      outside the changed files' blast radius;
    - many minutes / many tool turns without converging toward its
      final structured output;
    - a cost/budget warning event.
  The message: "Budget pressure: stop exploring and emit your
  review_output NOW with what you already have — findings at or above
  the severity floor only. A delivered review at current depth beats a
  dead run."

  Intervene AT MOST twice per run, then stay silent (decision: done).
  Never comment on the code itself, never suggest findings, never ask
  the reviewer to dig deeper.

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow review_pr:
  entry: diff_precheck

  ## A review READS the target repo; it never builds it. Installing the
  ## toolchain that repo pins to build ITSELF buys this run nothing and
  ## costs it a cold Nix realise before the first node — minutes on a
  ## large repo, long enough to outlast the window a sandbox has to come
  ## up. The bot's own devbox.json, if it ever ships one, still installs.
  ## Override per run with `--repo-devbox on`.
  repo_devbox: off

  ## The same fact, applied to the safety net: a review writes no commit
  ## for the repo it reads, so the mid-run workspace checkpoint has
  ## nothing of this run's to preserve — while its push would put an
  ## `iterion/run-<id>-checkpoint` BRANCH on that repo, carrying whatever
  ## the tree holds. This bot's own `.review-pr/` scratch is untracked and
  ## nothing ignores it, so `git add -A` sweeps the findings file in:
  ## measured 2026-09-08, an internal review whose board posting was
  ## deliberately off was published to a public repository that way.
  ## The findings a reader needs live in node outputs, which are durable
  ## in the store on their own.
  workspace_checkpoint: off

  budget:
    ## Two read-only reviewer branches run in parallel; emit runs alone
    ## afterwards.
    max_parallel_branches: 2
    ## The money guard is max_cost_usd; the duration cap only needs to
    ## catch a hung run. 45m killed real reviews mid-publish (a forfait
    ## retry or a big diff walks past it while spending nothing). The same
    ## 90% wall applies here, no grace: 90m refused new nodes at 81 min.
    ## The two deaths sized above ran ~40 min each (push → death: 84 min
    ## for two runs of one PR, 53 min for the other); a review funded to
    ## ~$43 may run 45-60 min, so 120m keeps its 108-minute wall clear of
    ## the workload the cost cap now admits.
    max_duration: "120m"
    ## Sized from production, twice. The 2026-09-03 sample said median
    ## ~$3.6/run, p95 ~$10.5, and the cap was 12 with the exit grace
    ## (cap × 1.1) walking an over-cap run forward to publish. Three days
    ## later the guard tier died at $36 and $30 on a 7-file PR — twice on
    ## one revision, the relaunch included — and the merge gate posted no
    ## verdict at all: a required check that dies is worse than one that
    ## costs. A review that reproduces its findings and answers the fixer's
    ## rounds is the workload now; the cap follows it.
    ##
    ## The EFFECTIVE ceiling is 0.9 × cap, not the cap: the engine refuses
    ## any new node — the publish tail included — once an axis sits at 90%
    ## of its cap, with no grace on that band (pkg/runtime/budget.go). And
    ## $36 was the spend at the kill, a lower bound on the workload, since
    ## the run never paid for converge + publish. 48 puts $36 at 75% and
    ## leaves ~$43 before the wall. Re-budget a deliberate deep review per
    ## run with --max-cost-usd.
    max_cost_usd: 48

  ## EFFICIENCY: an empty review diff skips tier resolution, both LLM
  ## reviewers, and emit.
  diff_precheck -> tier_expand when not is_empty
  diff_precheck -> done when is_empty

  ## REVIEW TIERS (#685): resolve severity_threshold / max_findings /
  ## post_to_board / effective_review_mode before topology routes.
  tier_expand -> topology

  ## TOPOLOGY (ADR-052 + review tiers). `effective_review_mode` (audit
  ## forces it regardless of review_mode) takes the fan-out and runs both
  ## FULL-strength families; glance routes to the lighter, cheaper
  ## same-family reviewer; guard/any other tier keeps the full-strength
  ## reviewer. The claude edge is the unconditional fallback, so an
  ## unset/unknown review_mode/review_tier degrades to the cheap path
  ## rather than to an empty router.
  topology -> fan when "vars.review_mode == 'dual' || vars.review_tier == 'audit'"
  topology -> reviewer_gpt_glance when "vars.review_tier == 'glance' && vars.mono_family == 'gpt'"
  topology -> reviewer_gpt when "vars.mono_family == 'gpt'"
  topology -> reviewer_claude_glance when "vars.review_tier == 'glance'"
  topology -> reviewer_claude

  ## Fan out to both FULL-strength family reviewers (dual only — audit's
  ## defining spend never routes through the glance variants).
  fan -> reviewer_claude
  fan -> reviewer_gpt

  ## Converge on emit (await: wait_all). emit reads
  ## outputs.merge_reviews (normalised per family) directly, so the
  ## edges carry no data mapping.
  reviewer_claude -> merge_reviews
  reviewer_gpt -> merge_reviews
  reviewer_claude_glance -> merge_reviews
  reviewer_gpt_glance -> merge_reviews
  merge_reviews -> converge

  ## emit always runs (board + markdown report). Then pr_gate decides
  ## whether to ALSO publish to a forge PR — only when pr_url is set —
  ## feeding emit's merged findings forward to the publish step.
  converge -> pr_gate with {
    pr_url: "{{vars.pr_url}}",
    findings: "{{outputs.converge.findings}}",
    questions: "{{outputs.converge.questions}}",
    ticket_conformance: "{{outputs.converge.ticket_conformance}}",
    total_findings: "{{outputs.converge.total_findings}}",
    claude_findings: "{{outputs.merge_reviews.claude_findings}}",
    gpt_findings: "{{outputs.merge_reviews.gpt_findings}}"
  }
  ## No PR target → finish after the board + report (the default,
  ## zero extra LLM cost). PR target → deterministic server-mediated
  ## forge publish (no LLM, no workspace credential).
  pr_gate -> done when not has_pr
  pr_gate -> publish_review when has_pr with {
    review_scope: "{{outputs.merge_reviews.review_scope}}",
    ## diff_precheck's ignorance sentinel (-1), mapped on the EDGE because a
    ## tool node's command body substitutes only input/vars/secrets/run.id —
    ## an {{outputs.…}} ref there reaches the shell as literal text.
    scope_files: "{{outputs.diff_precheck.changed_files}}",
    ai_run_tokens: "{{run.tokens}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_model: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude._model}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_backend: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude._backend}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_tokens: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude._tokens}}",
    ## The engine writes _fallback_used ONLY when the node was served by
    ## something other than its first choice, so its PRESENCE is the fact.
    ## The gate reads it instead of inferring degradation from whichever
    ## model name it happens to see.
    ai_reviewer_claude_fallback: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude._fallback_used}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_model: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt._model}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_backend: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt._backend}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_tokens: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt._tokens}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_glance_model: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude_glance._model}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_glance_backend: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude_glance._backend}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_glance_tokens: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude_glance._tokens}}",
    ai_reviewer_claude_glance_fallback: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude_glance._fallback_used}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_model: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance._model}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_backend: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance._backend}}",
    ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_tokens: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance._tokens}}",
    ai_converge_model: "{{outputs.converge._model}}",
    ai_converge_backend: "{{outputs.converge._backend}}",
    ai_converge_tokens: "{{outputs.converge._tokens}}",
    pr_url: "{{outputs.pr_gate.pr_url}}",
    reviewed_sha: "{{outputs.diff_precheck.reviewed_sha}}",
    effective_review_mode: "{{outputs.tier_expand.effective_review_mode}}",
    findings: "{{outputs.pr_gate.findings}}",
    questions: "{{outputs.pr_gate.questions}}",
    ticket_conformance: "{{outputs.pr_gate.ticket_conformance}}",
    total_findings: "{{outputs.pr_gate.total_findings}}",
    claude_findings: "{{outputs.pr_gate.claude_findings}}",
    gpt_findings: "{{outputs.pr_gate.gpt_findings}}"
  }
  ## Deterministic anti-façade gate on the publish result, then done.
  publish_review -> publish_health with {
    published: "{{outputs.publish_review.published}}",
    comments_posted: "{{outputs.publish_review.comments_posted}}",
    total_findings: "{{outputs.pr_gate.total_findings}}",
    skipped_reason: "{{outputs.publish_review.skipped_reason}}",
    review_url: "{{outputs.publish_review.review_url}}"
  }
  publish_health -> done
